# Build trees
build/
build-*/

# LaTeX intermediates. The PDF itself IS tracked -- it is the deliverable most
# people will read, and rebuilding it needs a full TeX install.
*.aux
*.log
*.out
*.toc
*.synctex.gz

# Raw field dumps consumed by doc/fig/*.py to make the figures. 21 MB of binary
# that would sit in history forever, and every one is regenerable by re-running
# its case with FIGDUMP=1 (or FIGVOL=1 / FIGVEC=1 for the aorta). The PNGs they
# produce ARE tracked, because doc/m3lb.tex needs them to build.
doc/fig/*.bin
*.ppm

# Stray run outputs
*.vti

# Third-party publisher PDFs. Kept on disk, deliberately not shipped:
# redistributing them is not ours to do.
SomeRefs/
.DS_Store

# Field dumps and animation frames under results/. These are regenerable by
# re-running the case (-field for tgv2d, -fevery for channel3d) and they are
# large: the channel's mid-plane contour dumps alone came to 59 MB against 24 kB
# for the profile tables beside them. The SUMMARY tables in results/ are tracked
# reference data; per-node field data is not.
results/*/field_*.dat
results/*/anim_*
results/I_channel/*_contour.dat
# Same rule for the Zhou et al. benchmarks: the raw float32 slices behind the
# contour figures are per-node data. The summary tables (conduction.dat,
# plate.dat, natural.dat, lid.dat) and the 1-D profile and centreline dumps
# beside them ARE tracked -- they are what the comparisons are read off.
results/J_zhou_thermal/*_temp.dat
results/J_zhou_thermal/*_ux.dat
results/J_zhou_thermal/*_uy.dat

# Rendered animations. Regenerable from doc/fig/*.py given a dump, and a dump
# is itself ignored -- so these are two steps from anything tracked. doc/m3lb.pdf
# is the tracked binary here because it is the deliverable; a video of a
# demonstrator is not.
doc/video/
GPU/build*/
